1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:03,630
Welcome back. In this video,

2
00:00:03,630 --> 00:00:07,215
we'll discuss how to tell a
powerful story using data.

3
00:00:07,215 --> 00:00:09,660
We'll teach you how to
gather and organize

4
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:11,505
your project data
to present it to

5
00:00:11,505 --> 00:00:13,815
others within your organization.

6
00:00:13,815 --> 00:00:16,770
Presenting is a powerful
way to communicate

7
00:00:16,770 --> 00:00:18,060
your ideas and support

8
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:20,505
your decisions throughout
the project journey.

9
00:00:20,505 --> 00:00:24,280
Think of presenting as telling
the story of your project.

10
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:27,140
Storytelling is the
process of turning facts

11
00:00:27,140 --> 00:00:30,565
into narrative to communicate
something to your audience.

12
00:00:30,565 --> 00:00:33,535
Storytelling is how you
bring data to life

13
00:00:33,535 --> 00:00:36,050
and is a useful way to
tell stakeholders within

14
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:38,975
your organization
about your project.

15
00:00:38,975 --> 00:00:41,900
In a way, we're all
storytellers using

16
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:45,590
information and experiences
to share ideas with others.

17
00:00:45,590 --> 00:00:49,655
Stories usually have a
beginning, middle, and end.

18
00:00:49,655 --> 00:00:51,830
To tell a great story using data,

19
00:00:51,830 --> 00:00:55,025
we'll go over some best
practices to make sure

20
00:00:55,025 --> 00:00:58,270
your story is complete,
accurate, and compelling.

21
00:00:58,270 --> 00:01:01,895
In general, there are six
main steps to storytelling.

22
00:01:01,895 --> 00:01:04,250
First, define your audience.

23
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:06,290
Then, collect the data.

24
00:01:06,290 --> 00:01:09,010
Next, filter and
analyze the data.

25
00:01:09,010 --> 00:01:12,215
Fourth, choose a
visual representation.

26
00:01:12,215 --> 00:01:13,970
Then, shape the story.

27
00:01:13,970 --> 00:01:16,495
And finally, gather
your feedback.

28
00:01:16,495 --> 00:01:18,840
Now let's review each one.

29
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:21,960
Step 1: define your audience.

30
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:23,585
In this first step,

31
00:01:23,585 --> 00:01:26,200
it's important to know
who you're presenting to.

32
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:28,230
Are you presenting
to project sponsors

33
00:01:28,230 --> 00:01:31,005
or executives? Or
to team members?

34
00:01:31,005 --> 00:01:32,990
Define your audience and find

35
00:01:32,990 --> 00:01:35,185
out what matters most to them.

36
00:01:35,185 --> 00:01:38,540
Begin by asking yourself
qualifying questions like:

37
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:41,390
What would my audience want
to know about the project?

38
00:01:41,390 --> 00:01:44,495
Or, what are their
most urgent concerns?

39
00:01:44,495 --> 00:01:46,175
Which key data points

40
00:01:46,175 --> 00:01:48,665
influence the story
and project outcome?

41
00:01:48,665 --> 00:01:50,360
This will set you up to know

42
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:51,740
the type of story you want to

43
00:01:51,740 --> 00:01:55,055
tell and the type of data
you should use to tell it.

44
00:01:55,055 --> 00:01:57,425
For example, years ago,

45
00:01:57,425 --> 00:01:59,795
I was working on a
project for Google Maps.

46
00:01:59,795 --> 00:02:01,910
Our goal was to apply a label to

47
00:02:01,910 --> 00:02:04,100
every business in the
world: restaurants,

48
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:06,370
hotels, gas stations—you
name it.

49
00:02:06,370 --> 00:02:07,655
There are many, many,

50
00:02:07,655 --> 00:02:09,395
many businesses in the world,

51
00:02:09,395 --> 00:02:12,455
and I only had a small team
of engineers to work with.

52
00:02:12,455 --> 00:02:15,290
For this project, we
tailored our storytelling to

53
00:02:15,290 --> 00:02:16,940
an audience of vice presidents

54
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:19,250
from Google Maps and Google Search.

55
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:21,095
Both were important to consider,

56
00:02:21,095 --> 00:02:23,390
because we were
helping users find and

57
00:02:23,390 --> 00:02:24,860
connect to businesses through

58
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:27,695
the Google Maps app and
through Google Search.

59
00:02:27,695 --> 00:02:29,630
I'll return to this
example as I take

60
00:02:29,630 --> 00:02:31,745
you through the steps
of storytelling.

61
00:02:31,745 --> 00:02:33,515
Next step is Step 2:

62
00:02:33,515 --> 00:02:35,240
Find the data that connects to

63
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:36,950
the question you want to answer.

64
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:39,530
You'll need to begin
searching for data from

65
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:41,540
trustworthy resources to support

66
00:02:41,540 --> 00:02:43,270
the point you're trying to make.

67
00:02:43,270 --> 00:02:45,650
Leverage the relevant
project resources

68
00:02:45,650 --> 00:02:47,870
in documents like
your project plan or

69
00:02:47,870 --> 00:02:49,520
work management software to

70
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:52,805
download and analyze
key data points.

71
00:02:52,805 --> 00:02:54,905
For my Google Maps project,

72
00:02:54,905 --> 00:02:57,110
the question we were
trying to answer was:

73
00:02:57,110 --> 00:02:59,840
Where should we focus
our attention first?

74
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:02,165
So to find the right
data for this question,

75
00:03:02,165 --> 00:03:03,830
we turned to the many businesses

76
00:03:03,830 --> 00:03:05,510
in our internal database and

77
00:03:05,510 --> 00:03:07,640
the available information
about what types

78
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:10,630
of businesses users
were searching for.

79
00:03:10,630 --> 00:03:13,335
That brings us to Step 3:

80
00:03:13,335 --> 00:03:15,675
Filter and analyze the data.

81
00:03:15,675 --> 00:03:17,840
Now that you've
collected your data,

82
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:18,920
you'll need to vet it for

83
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:21,410
credibility and filter
the information.

84
00:03:21,410 --> 00:03:22,820
For my Maps project,

85
00:03:22,820 --> 00:03:25,460
we used search queries to
determine the types of

86
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:28,475
businesses that users
searched for most often,

87
00:03:28,475 --> 00:03:31,385
which included restaurants,
cafes, and hotels.

88
00:03:31,385 --> 00:03:33,225
There were other categories—gas stations,

89
00:03:33,225 --> 00:03:35,395
museums, etc.—but

90
00:03:35,395 --> 00:03:37,070
they collectively made up

91
00:03:37,070 --> 00:03:41,365
a much smaller percentage of
geo-specific search traffic.

92
00:03:41,365 --> 00:03:45,135
Step 4: Choose a
visual representation.

93
00:03:45,135 --> 00:03:48,895
Visualizations are a great
way to help people remember

94
00:03:48,895 --> 00:03:50,840
the information
you're presenting and

95
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:53,585
are an essential piece
of storytelling.

96
00:03:53,585 --> 00:03:56,210
You can use data in
different ways to tell

97
00:03:56,210 --> 00:03:58,280
a story, like using dashboards,

98
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:00,670
charts, infographics,
and mappings,

99
00:04:00,670 --> 00:04:02,210
and we'll go over these examples

100
00:04:02,210 --> 00:04:04,375
in more detail in the next video.

101
00:04:04,375 --> 00:04:06,110
For my Maps project,

102
00:04:06,110 --> 00:04:08,720
we decided on a pie chart
to help tell our story,

103
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:12,050
which brings me to Step
5: Shape the story.

104
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:14,030
After you've analyzed
your data and

105
00:04:14,030 --> 00:04:15,725
know how you'd like
to visualize it,

106
00:04:15,725 --> 00:04:17,495
it's time to tie it all together

107
00:04:17,495 --> 00:04:19,730
into one cohesive narrative.

108
00:04:19,730 --> 00:04:21,410
Take some time to think about

109
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:22,850
what you're hoping to achieve,

110
00:04:22,850 --> 00:04:24,185
the points you want to make,

111
00:04:24,185 --> 00:04:26,660
and the questions and
concerns you want to answer.

112
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:28,175
For the Maps project,

113
00:04:28,175 --> 00:04:30,170
we used the pie
chart to show that

114
00:04:30,170 --> 00:04:32,720
most geo-specific
search queries are

115
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:36,035
covered by a relatively
small number of businesses.

116
00:04:36,035 --> 00:04:38,785
So we built a story
about that data.

117
00:04:38,785 --> 00:04:42,170
We wanted agreement from our
VPs to work on improving

118
00:04:42,170 --> 00:04:43,700
the data behind this set of

119
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:45,890
categories in a
few major markets.

120
00:04:45,890 --> 00:04:47,900
It needed to illustrate that

121
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:50,045
if we improved the category data,

122
00:04:50,045 --> 00:04:52,580
we'd succeed in improving
the search results for

123
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:55,615
more than 50% of geo searches.

124
00:04:55,615 --> 00:04:59,630
Lastly is Step 6:
Gather your feedback.

125
00:04:59,630 --> 00:05:02,015
Similar to how you
may ask a friend

126
00:05:02,015 --> 00:05:04,200
to practice with you
before an interview,

127
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:06,515
you want to be sure that
before you present,

128
00:05:06,515 --> 00:05:08,450
you do a trial run.

129
00:05:08,450 --> 00:05:10,520
Try getting feedback from someone

130
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:12,520
who's not connected
to the project.

131
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:14,265
Find out if it was interesting.

132
00:05:14,265 --> 00:05:17,355
Did it make sense? What
questions did they have?

133
00:05:17,355 --> 00:05:19,040
Their feedback can help you

134
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:21,170
identify areas of
your story that were

135
00:05:21,170 --> 00:05:23,450
unclear or unmemorable and

136
00:05:23,450 --> 00:05:25,954
give you a final chance
to make adjustments.

137
00:05:25,954 --> 00:05:28,010
To recap, you want to define

138
00:05:28,010 --> 00:05:29,855
your audience, collect the data,

139
00:05:29,855 --> 00:05:31,630
filter and analyze the data,

140
00:05:31,630 --> 00:05:33,620
choose a visual representation,

141
00:05:33,620 --> 00:05:37,115
shape the story, and finally,
gather your feedback.

142
00:05:37,115 --> 00:05:39,665
The key to effective
storytelling is to be

143
00:05:39,665 --> 00:05:42,595
organized, intentional,
and prepared.

144
00:05:42,595 --> 00:05:45,480
Coming up, we'll talk
more about Step 4:

145
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:48,910
effective visualization.
See you there.
